{0 Parseff}

Parseff is a direct-style parser combinator library for OCaml 5. Parsers are
plain functions ([unit -> 'a]); the runtime handles backtracking, streaming
input, and error reporting with source excerpts. Designed for performance with
zero-copy slice APIs and fused operations.

{{:https://davesnx.github.io/parseff/}Documentation}

{1 Installation}

{@bash skip[
$ opam install parseff -y
]}

{1 Example}

{@ocaml[
type Parseff.custom_error += Out_of_range of int

let octet () =
  let digits = Parseff.many ~at_least:1 Parseff.digit in
  let value d = Char.code d - Char.code '0' in
  let n = List.fold_left (fun acc d -> (acc * 10) + value d) 0 digits in
  if n >= 0 && n <= 255 then n
  else Parseff.error (Out_of_range n)

let ip_address () =
  let a = octet () in
  let _ = Parseff.char '.' in
  let b = octet () in
  let _ = Parseff.char '.' in
  let c = octet () in
  let _ = Parseff.char '.' in
  let d = octet () in
  (a, b, c, d)

let () =
  match Parseff.parse "192.168.1.1" ip_address with
  | Ok (a, b, c, d) ->
      Printf.printf "Parsed: %d.%d.%d.%d\n" a b c d
  | Error { kind = Custom (Out_of_range n); range; _ } ->
      Printf.printf "Error at %d: %d out of range (0-255)\n" range.start n
  | Error e ->
      prerr_endline (Parseff.render_error ~name:"address" e)
]}

This prints [Parsed: 192.168.1.1]. On ["192.168.1."], [Parseff.render_error]
prints:

{v
address:1:11: [error] expected digit, found end of input
1 | 192.168.1.
  |           ^
v}

{1 Features}

- {b Direct-style parsers}: compose plain [unit -> 'a] functions without
  monadic ([>>=], [>>|], [*>]) or binding ([let*], [let+], [and+]) operators.
- {{:https://davesnx.github.io/parseff/api/errors/}{b Structured errors and source excerpts}}:
  furthest-failure reporting with merged expectations, typed custom errors,
  construct contexts, and multi-error
  {{:https://davesnx.github.io/parseff/guides/error-recovery/}recovery}.
- {b Automatic backtracking}: {{:https://davesnx.github.io/parseff/api/combinators/#or_}[Parseff.or_]}
  and [Parseff.one_of], with [commit] and [cut] to seal branches.
- {{:https://davesnx.github.io/parseff/api/streaming/}{b Pull and push streaming}}:
  [Source.parse], [Source.parse_each], and [Push] with bounded backtracking.
- {b Domain-safe}: parse state lives per domain so independent parses run in
  parallel across OCaml 5 domains.
- {{:https://davesnx.github.io/parseff/api/zero-copy/}{b Zero-copy slices and fused scanners}}:
  [Slice.t], [Charset], and [sep_by_take] for low-allocation hot paths.
- {b Minimal dependencies}: depends only on [uucp] for Unicode character
  properties.

{1 Performance}

Parseff is built for throughput and low allocation: in the benchmark suite,
Parseff's generic parsers are up to ~1.5x faster than Angstrom's baselines, and
its fused zero-copy paths are ~3x faster. Against Angstrom's optimized JSON
parser, Parseff's optimized path is ~1.5x faster while reducing minor
allocations from ~11.1 GB to ~1.3 GB.

See the {{:https://davesnx.github.io/parseff/guides/comparison/}comparison with Angstrom},
{{:https://github.com/davesnx/parseff/blob/main/bench/bench_json.ml}[bench/bench_json.ml]},
and {{:https://github.com/davesnx/parseff/tree/main/bench}[bench/]}.

